hard disk boot on the simulated Decision 1 - where this stands
=============================================================

HWSIM.STATUS

The goal was to stop hand-stamping the simulator's drive images and do it
the way the hardware did: boot CP/M, format an m5 with Morrow's own
FORMATMW, install Micronix onto what that produced, and boot it from the
hard disk.  All of that has been made to work once, end to end.  It does
not work at the time of writing, and the last section says what is
broken.


what works, and how to do it
----------------------------

Format.  CP/M boots from disks/dist/1009-8_cold-boot.IMD:

	d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -d <drivedir> \
	   1009-8_cold-boot.IMD 947-1.IMD

That disk's FORMATMW is version 1.6, which predates the part number
names - "m5" is rejected there, "seagate" and "st506" are the same drive
and do work.  947-1.IMD carries 1.7 with the full option set, so it goes
in as B: and the format is

	B:FORMATMW M5 SIZE 512

512 because Micronix requires it; see src/hwsim/DISKS.  The whole drive
formats and verifies in about six seconds.

Install.  From disks/hdinstall, with the 1.67 standalone on the 5 1/4
port, switches 0x14 (0x10 djdma floppy boot, 0x04 skip the monitor):

	d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -c 0x14 -d <drivedir> -5 sa67.5.skew.1k

and at the shell prompt, a script like m5init but using the new mkfs:

	mkfs -i /bootmw.bin /dev/m5a -1024
	fsck /dev/m5a
	mount /dev/m5a /b
	cptree / /b -v
	...
	setdev /b/micronix 3/0 0/0

Boot.  Switches 0x0c - 0x08 selects nuboot, which is the HDC-DMA path,
plus 0x04 to skip the monitor.  The rom's decode is in mon447.s at tstsw:
0x00 is boothd and that is the HDCA, not this controller.

	d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -c 0x0c -d <drivedir>

What that produced, once:

	mwboot:
	Micronix loader for the HD-DMA
	Files:
	micronix
	Loading
	Entering

	Micronix 1.67
	...
	disks: djdma hddma
	root dev: hddma/0
	swap dev: nodev/0
	#


reference values worth not rediscovering
----------------------------------------

An m5 (= seagate, st506) at 512 bytes, from FORMATMW's own table and
arithmetic: 153 cylinders, 4 heads, 17 sectors, 10404 blocks, 5326848
bytes.  The drive label after a real format reads

	secsize 512  cyl 153  heads 4  spt 17
	firstsec 0  seccode 3  gap3 43  fill 0xe5  formatted 1

The format command carries gap3 and the sector count complemented -
0xd5 is 43, 0xee is 17 - and its size code is the complement of the
specify command's, so 512 is 0xfc to format and 3 to specify.  The
sector id table it points at is FOUR bytes an entry: cylinder low,
cylinder high, head, sector, with sectors numbered from zero and
interleaved by the skew.  Confirmed on the wire.

Read Headers returns EIGHT bytes - mark, 0xfe, cylinder low, cylinder
high, head, sector, two CRC - per the HDCDMA technical manual 2.2.3.
Byte 4 is the head, which is what stand/mwio.c's probe tests.  The
command takes no sector argument; it answers with whatever comes round
next.

sys/mw.c does not map block 0 to cylinder 0.  It rotates:

	cyl = blk / spc;  cyl += tracks >> 1;  if (cyl >= tracks) cyl -= tracks;

so cylinder 0 is block 5236 on the 5 meg, which is where mkfs -i puts
the boot area.  Getting this wrong writes a boot into the middle of a
filesystem and says it worked.

The loader has 4k and there is no way around it.  The kernel loads at
1000 and takes everything above; 0000-0fff is the supervisor's rom and
I/O page.  Everything in stand/boot.c - text, data and bss - must end
below 0ff0.  See the header comment there.


what was wrong, and is now fixed
--------------------------------

In the simulator:

  hddma.c recorded the format wrongly.  firstsec was hardcoded 1 when
  sectors are numbered from 0, the size code was stored in the format
  command's complement rather than the specify form, and gap3 was stored
  complemented - so a header read back one sector high with a nonsense
  gap.  The id table dump read 8 bytes an entry instead of 4.  The
  "command.sseccode = 3" line, which forced every specify to 512, is
  gone: CP/M formats at 1024 and both have to work.

  drive_header returned five bytes of its own invention.  It now returns
  the manual's eight, with a rotating sector so successive reads walk the
  track, and refuses a head or cylinder the drive does not have - which
  is what makes a probe mean anything.

  lib/harddisk.c is lib/hdcdmadisk.c.  The geometry would suit any drive
  but the header format is this controller's.  Watch for a stale
  harddisk.o left inside libsim.a; it can win at link time.

In micronix:

  cmd/mkfs used bootfirst at line 442 and computed it at line 487, so
  the in-use check read whatever that held - 58626 on a 10404 block
  drive - and died.

  stand/boot.c loaded nothing: loadsize is text + data + 0x10, which for
  this kernel is 59633 and reads as -5903 in a signed 16 bit int, so the
  load loop never ran and it entered an unloaded kernel.

  stand/boot.c walked the block list in indirbuf, which straddles the
  load address, so the load overwrote the list it was reading.  The list
  is copied to blist first.

  stand/sexit.s answers crt0's "jp sexit" so the library's exit - and the
  stdio it flushes - stays out.  Worth 618 bytes of text and 578 of bss,
  which is the difference between fitting under 0ff0 and not.

  stand/mwboot1.s set the stack to RELOC, 0c000, which is in the middle
  of where a 117 block kernel lands.  It is at the top of memory now.

  cmd/ddt patching of the kernel's root device is replaced by
  tools/setdev, which reads _rootdev and _swapdev out of the kernel's own
  symbol table.  The old recipe searched for the bytes of djdma/12, which
  is 1.6's root device; 1.67 has djdma/76, so it silently did nothing and
  every disk built that way booted a kernel looking for the floppy.

In ccc, which is a separate tree:

  K&R array parameters and union initializers were both fixed there.
  Byte-wide switch case labels are a documented restriction now - see
  ccc/src/RESTRICTIONS.md - which is why formatmw switches on size/128.


what is broken now
------------------

Booting from the hard disk fails at the loader with

	Files:
	No bootable files

The head probe is sending the same command eleven times.  Every read
header in the trace is byte for byte identical:

	readhead ... step: 65535 down head: 0 args 1e 0 64 3 dmaaddr: 0x80

cmd.headsel is not changing across the loop, so the controller rightly
answers head 0 every time, the probe matches only i = 0, gives up at
i = 1, and sets spec.heads and spec.spc to something wrong.  Every block
number after that lands on the wrong cylinder and head, and the block it
read as the root directory was not the root directory.

Three things ruled out:

  Not the 32 entry directory limit.  The root directory is 352 bytes,
  22 entries, one block.  (Reading more of it would not cost buffer
  space either - it reuses dirbuf - only the code to walk d_addr.)

  Not the source cleanup that removed the __STDC__ and INIT conditionals.
  The pre-cleanup loader fails the same way on the same disk.

  Not the disk.  The image that booted and the image that does not have
  byte identical root inodes, and identical inodes for /micronix -
  IALLOC|ILARG, addr[0] 812.

So something differs between the run that booted and the runs since, and
it has not been found.  One unexplained detail that may be the thread to
pull: in the trace of the run that booted, arg1 of the read header
command is 9, and in the failing runs it is 0.


the trees
---------

This work is spread across two working copies and none of it is
committed.  The loader and simulator changes - boot.c, mwio.c,
mwboot1.s, sexit.s, hddma.c, hdcdmadisk.c, mkfs.c, setdev.c, formatmw.c -
exist BOTH as uncommitted changes in the main checkout and as copies in
the worktree, which was branched from origin/master and then reset onto
hwsim-drives.  Reconcile these before doing more, or one set will be lost.

src/hwsim/d1/FORMATMW.C is the clean 1.7 source recovered from
SOURCES/202/FORMATMW.BAK, with the 1.8 proto drive table merged in.  The
copies that were being used had one line of NewWord damage - high bit set
on the last letter of each word - and the missing semicolon after gap3[]
is original to the source, not damage.

stand/formatmw.c is that source ported off the Whitesmiths library.  It
builds with ccc -m cpm.  It has not been run.


driving the simulator without a person
--------------------------------------

The simulator wants a controlling terminal, so a script that drives it
has to give it a pty - python's os.forkpty and an expect loop is enough.
Two things to know if you write one:

  Match on something that can only appear in the command's OUTPUT.  The
  shell echoes what was typed, and the prompt is already on the screen,
  so patterns like "# " or the command's own words match before the
  command has run.  Ignore everything up to the point of the send.

  A run takes as long as it takes.  cptree is five minutes and fsck's
  bad block hunt is longer; a per-expect timeout of five minutes reads
  as the simulator exiting on its own part way through the install.
